{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-compiler-beta-release","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Did React Compiler originally require React 19, and how was backward compatibility handled?","answer":"Yes, it originally depended on runtime APIs added in React 19; the team later added support for it to also work with React 17 and 18."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19","statement":"React v19 became available as a stable release on npm on December 5, 2024.","source_span":"December 05, 2024 by [The React Team]"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19","statement":"React 19 adds a new hook called useActionState.","source_span":"we've added a new hook called `useActionState`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19","statement":"React.useActionState was previously called ReactDOM.useFormState in Canary releases, and useFormState has been deprecated.","source_span":"was previously called `ReactDOM.useFormState` in the Canary releases, but we've renamed it and deprecated `useFormState`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19","statement":"By convention, functions that use async transitions in React 19 are called \"Actions\", which automatically manage pending state, optimistic updates, error handling, and forms.","source_span":"By convention, functions that use async transitions are called \"Actions\""}
